  "answer": "The goal was met: five settings on five different scenes failed every time they were repeated (S1, S4, S13, S22, S23). But none of these failures, and no failure anywhere in the study, involved the pedestrian. In every case the policy drifted left into a neighbouring lane, a parked or oncoming car, or off the drivable area. Several of these scenes also failed at other speeds and step-out times, so the failures don't come from a particular combination of ego speed and pedestrian timing. With so few repeats, each confirmed setting is only shown to fail more often than not; its true rate could be anywhere from just over half to always. Two of the five need caveats. Every failure at S1 is flagged as a possible simulator artifact, though the videos show the policy at fault. Two of the three S23 failures rest on an off-road flag that may be marginal.",
